"""Meetings M2–M17 (Weeks 1–4): Studios 1–4, the front of the research arc.

Since D49 the weekly spine IS the book's Studio arc, one Studio per week:
Monday and Wednesday teach that Studio's EDR|AI lessons together with their
"It is your turn" work, and Friday IS that Studio's milestone, built in the
room and submitted at the close.

Week 1 (nb01) — Studio 1, frame the inquiry. Monday is the D49 exception: the
instructor-led orientation, the one lecture that is not a book lesson (the
course promise, the one-Studio-per-week architecture, the milestone chain, the
lab-meeting system, Colab and dataset readiness, and the modeled unscored Case 464
audit). Wednesday teaches curiosity-to-problem. Week 2 (nb02) — Studio 2,
govern the work: Monday runs the four governance lessons as one arc (arm not
brain, research director, the seven moves, responsibility and ownership),
Wednesday the two question lessons (kind x reach, and declaring the question).
Week 3 (nb03) — Studio 3, ground it in verified evidence: SHORT WEEK (no class
Mon Sep 7, Labor Day), so a single Wednesday lecture carries both evidence
lessons. Week 4 (nb04) — Studio 4, declare and diagnose provisionally: Monday
MIDA + uncertainty foundations, Wednesday declare-diagnose + ethics and data
governance.

Course milestone M(n) presents book Milestone (n+1): M1 -> Book Milestone 1,
M2 -> 2, M3 -> 3, M4 -> 4. Fridays are studios (stand-up + the milestone kicked
off from its brief, worked with the student's AI assistant, and submitted). The
lab meeting opens every Mon/Wed lecture from meeting 4 onward, and it is the
same open round every time, so the marker field carries one identical string;
Week 1's two lectures are instructor-led and open the round from the
instructor's chair, so their marker field stays empty.

D74 retired the graded Student Research Lead role and replaced its opening block
with a ten-minute LAB MEETING; D75 then withdrew the assignment inside it. There
is no reporter, no slot draw, no assigned question and no preparation of any kind
by anyone. The instructor asks the room how the projects are going — what was
decided since last time, what the evidence looks like, where somebody is stuck —
and the room answers. Nothing said in the lab meeting is graded, and from minute
10 the instructor leads the lesson and owns accuracy, the AI tooling, and the
clock. The frames are Monday 10/21/12/7 and Wednesday 10/20/12/8. The column keys
are UNCHANGED for compatibility: `srl_slot` now carries the LAB-MEETING MARKER
for that lecture — one identical string wherever it is set, since nothing
distinguishes one lecture's round from another's — and `srl_focus` the
instructor-owned puzzle or challenge that opens the lecture's own block from
minute 10. The SRL material itself is kept on disk, retired in place for a future
edition, and so is the withdrawn slot draw.

The Synthetic Colleague audits are SCORED in Weeks 2–10 and Week 12, always
inside a Monday puzzle or a Wednesday laboratory, never on a Friday and never
as homework. Week 1 carries the modeled, UNSCORED Case 464 instead.

RDSS chapter titles verified against book.declaredesign.org; never invent a
chapter. AI is named generically (your AI assistant) in student-facing
language; GenAI Studio reviewer roles appear at their designated milestones.
No fabricated citations anywhere — the seeded colleague flaws misuse real
sources, they never invent one.
"""

